  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1054 PM CST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\nAnalysis this evening showed a 500 mb low spinning over the \nMO/AR border with a band of heavy snow falling along the MO/IL \nborder and extending north into southeast IA. As the low \ncontinues to push southeast then east overnight, some wrap-\naround moisture will push westward toward the forecast area, but\nguidance remains in very good agreement that any precipitation \nwill stay several counties to our east. Instead, surface ridging\nwill remain in control for us, with mostly clear skies and \nlight winds leading to one of the colder nights of the season so\nfar. Expect lows in the teens to lower 20s. Also will want to \nkeep an eye on patchy fog potential, with a few pieces of \nguidance hinting at some lower visibility with model soundings \nshowing a decently deep light wind layer, especially over \nnortheast NE. If any fog does develop, a few slick spots will be\npossible given the temperatures.\n\nThanksgiving will be similar to Wednesday with the cool surface\nridging still in place. Expect light winds and a decent amount \nof sunshine, but temperatures will once again only reach the \nupper 30s to lower 40s.\n\nSoutherly surface flow and a brief period of upper level \nridging will lead to slightly warmer weather on Friday, with \nhighs in the mid 40s to lower 50s. By Friday evening into early \nSaturday, some weak shortwave energy and a surface trough are \nprogged to slide through the area, potentially bringing some \nspotty light rain to a few locations (5-10% chance), though \nthere will be quite a bit of dry air to overcome.\n\nHigher and more widespread precip chances arrive for Saturday \nafternoon into early Sunday as a much stronger shortwave trough \nand cold front push in while a surface low spins up over the \nSouthern Plains. With daytime temperatures in the 40s to lower \n50s, precip will start as rain, but as cold air surges in from \nthe north Saturday night, a little snow may mix in. While there \nremains spread in track and timing of the system, there's decent\nagreement that any snow accumulations will remain minimal. \nHowever, with liquid precipitation amounts approaching a quarter\ninch, some of that lingering moisture on roads could freeze \novernight as temperatures look to bottom out just below freezing\nin areas near and north of I-80. The good news is that \ntemperatures look to climb back above freezing by mid-morning \nSunday, so if there are any impacts from slick roads, they \nshould diminish pretty quickly. Temperatures Sunday will top out\nin the 40s.\n\nConsensus favors quiet weather Sunday into early next week with\nnorthwesterly flow aloft becoming more zonal. However, there \nare signs of at least a weak shortwave trough sliding through \nsometime Monday night into Tuesday which could bring at least \nsome light precip. Chances currently remain under 15% though. \nOtherwise, we should see a gradual warming trend into mid-week, \nwith highs getting back into the mid 40s to lower 50s.",
